theory.intro:
A **cubic inequality** compares a cubic to $0$. Move every term to one side, factorise, then find where the curve is above or below the $x$-axis. The roots of $P(x) = 0$ are the **critical values** that split the number line.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-4.6,0) -- (4.6,0);
\node[right] at (4.7,0) {$x$};
\foreach \x in {-2.5,0,2.5} \draw (\x,-0.15) -- (\x,0.15);
\node[below] at (-2.5,-0.2) {$\alpha$};
\node[below] at (0,-0.2) {$\beta$};
\node[below] at (2.5,-0.2) {$\gamma$};
\node at (-3.6,0.55) {$-$};
\node at (-1.25,0.55) {$+$};
\node at (1.25,0.55) {$-$};
\node at (3.6,0.55) {$+$};
\node at (0,1.3) {sign of $P(x)$ when $a > 0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
At a **simple root** $(x - c)^1$ the curve crosses the $x$-axis, so the sign of $P(x)$ changes there.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-2.2,0) -- (2.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-1.7:1.7, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.25*\x*(\x*\x+1)});
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\node[below] at (0,-0.2) {$c$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

At a **double root** $(x - c)^2$ the curve touches the axis without crossing, so the sign of $P(x)$ does not change.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-2.2,0) -- (2.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-1.5:1.5, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.7*\x*\x});
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\node[below] at (0,-0.2) {$c$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
